<.field_report
  signed_in?={@signed_in?}
  title="How ChatGPT Sol and Terra used emisar to trace and fix a website outage"
  category="Website outage"
  intro="A gaming website kept timing out while its application processes were still running. I used emisar to compare running instances, service health, metrics, and logs. That evidence narrowed the investigation to page rendering, where source inspection revealed repeated CDN downloads and a missing server cache. The fix is now deployed and verified."
  stats={[
    {"The root cause found in", "5 minutes"},
    {"Fix shipped in", "17 minutes"}
  ]}
>
  <.field_report_section
    id="section-1"
    label="How I worked"
    title="Choose the next check from the live evidence"
  >
    <p>
      Across the recurring incidents leading up to the cache fix, I sent 126 action requests through emisar. Those were repeated rounds of public probes, service checks, metrics, and logs. Each round answered a question that determined what I checked next.
    </p>
    <p>
      I used <code>find_actions</code>
      to search for the checks I needed, then <code>get_action</code>
      to read their inputs, limits, and side effects. For each <code>run_action</code>
      request, I selected the action, pack version, and runner, and supplied the reason, supporting evidence, and expected result. The same MCP connection let me move from a slow public page to the services and hosts behind it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site ran across five Nomad allocations, the running instances of its website job. Requests passed through the CDN and Traefik before reaching an HAProxy sidecar and the Node application. From there, rendering a page could mean calling another service or downloading static game data.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section
    id="section-2"
    label="Stage 1"
    title="Follow a slow page into the application"
  >
    <p>
      I started with <code>net.http_probe</code>
      from two runners. DNS, connection setup, and TLS took milliseconds, but a public page took about ten seconds to start responding. Another path hit the 15-second timeout. One probe received HTTP 200 and still failed to finish downloading the page. The status code alone hid what a visitor experienced.
    </p>
    <p>
      Next I compared running processes with usable service instances. I requested
      <code>nomad.job_allocations</code>
      and paired it with <code>consul.service_passing_only</code>.
      Nomad listed five running instances; Consul returned only one passing instance. The other processes were alive but struggling to answer health checks.
    </p>
    <p>
      With <code>vm.query_range</code>, I compared the previous 45 minutes of proxy sessions, memory, request latency, and errors. All five public HAProxy frontends reached their 1,000-session limit while resident memory stayed around 2% of the allocation. That ruled out adding proxy memory as a useful response to those waiting connections.
    </p>
    <p>
      I then used <code>vm.query_instant</code>
      to split the workload by route. Champion pages held hundreds of requests open and took roughly 11–13 seconds at the 95th percentile. The busy
      <code>/api/auth/me</code>
      endpoint was still fast. That comparison focused the investigation on page rendering and its dependencies, while leaving authentication alone.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section
    id="incident-history"
    label="Stage 2"
    title="Reconstruct the outage after the site recovers"
  >
    <p>
      An earlier change shortened HAProxy's idle connection lifetime. When the outage returned, I first checked whether that change had reached production. Through emisar,
      <code>nomad.job_status_one</code>
      reported a successful deployment, and <code>nomad.job_allocations</code>
      showed all five instances on the updated job version. The next investigation could start from a confirmed recurrence after deployment.
    </p>
    <p>
      By then, fresh public probes were fast and Consul showed all five instances passing. I needed the state during the outage. I widened
      <code>vm.query_range</code>
      to six hours at five-minute intervals, then used timestamped <code>vm.query_instant</code>
      queries to inspect the incident itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those checks compared proxy sessions, backend queues, denied connections, application requests in flight, and route latency at the same point in time. The proxies had filled up even though their backend queues and denied-connection counters stayed at zero. I followed the requests into the application and its upstream services.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section
    id="incident-logs"
    label="Stage 3"
    title="Turn the log evidence into a sequence of failures"
  >
    <p>
      I used <code>vl.query</code>
      with <code>service</code>
      labels to query the website, Data API, and Traefik separately. The Data API sample showed
      <code>pool_busy</code>
      errors: both replicas had exhausted all 512 database query slots.
    </p>
    <p>
      I used <code>vl.stats_query</code>
      to count those errors by host: 1,967 on one replica and 159 on the other. Then
      <code>vl.stats_query_range</code>
      placed them in a three-minute burst. Earlier in the incident, each replica had logged 45 missing-data refresh warnings over three minutes. That gave me a concrete reason to inspect the refresh logic.
    </p>
    <p>
      Back in <code>vm.query_range</code>, I queried one aggregate series at a time using one-minute samples. That aligned Data API latency, upstream 503s, website requests in flight, HAProxy saturation, and ingress errors. Website requests in flight rose from six to 980 as the proxies filled; ingress errors followed in the next sample. The checks connected an upstream failure to what visitors saw.
    </p>
    <p>
      That explained an earlier recurrence, so database pool exhaustion was worth checking when the site failed again. In the final investigation, I also used direct infrastructure tools and application source reads. This time the pools had room: one replica had peaked at 359 of 512 slots; the other at 40. Page routes were still waiting, which sent the investigation further into the rendering code.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section id="section-3" label="Stage 4" title="Find the missing server cache">
    <p>
      With the live checks pointing toward page rendering, I followed that path in the application code. It led to static game data. Data Dragon is Riot's collection of League of Legends data: champions, items, runes, and similar information. The website fetched a copy from a CDN while building pages on the server. This is server-side rendering, or SSR: the server prepares the page before sending it to the browser.
    </p>
    <p>
      The code declared a two-hour cache, but that cache covered the desktop and browser path. Web SSR bypassed it.
    </p>
    <p>
      When the CDN slowed down, each page request could start a fetch for data another request was already fetching. More visitors arrived, more fetches started, and open connections accumulated across the five website processes. Some layouts started several static-data requests for each page.
    </p>
    <p>
      Raising the proxy limit would have allowed more requests to wait. The code change needed to reduce the repeated work.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section
    id="section-4"
    label="Stage 5"
    title="Share the fetch and keep a usable copy"
  >
    <p>
      I added a cache around the server's Data Dragon reads. A successful response stays fresh for two hours. During that time, page requests use the saved value. After it becomes stale, the server can keep serving it for up to one day while one refresh runs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Concurrent requests for the same key share that refresh. This is often called single-flight: if a fetch is already running, another request joins it instead of starting a duplicate. On a cold cache, requests still need the first response, but they share the work.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cache lives inside each website worker. Each worker can fetch its own copy; it does not need another service to coordinate all five workers. That keeps the change small while removing duplicate fetches within each process.
    </p>
    <p>
      I kept the scope to static Data Dragon data. Authentication, account information, and general API calls kept their existing behavior. Serving yesterday's item description during a CDN failure has a different consequence from serving stale account data.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section
    id="section-5"
    label="Stage 6"
    title="Deploy and verify the cache fix"
  >
    <p>
      The fix shipped in 17 minutes. It passed the full release pipeline: type checks, unit tests, lint, end-to-end tests, the image build, and asset upload. Release automation opened the deployment pull request with the production image ready.
    </p>
    <p>
      The deployment updated the website image without increasing the number of allocations or raising HAProxy's connection limit. The cache fix is now deployed and verified in production. Requests within each worker share the static-data fetch, and a usable cached copy remains available while it refreshes.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section
    id="agent-setup"
    label="Agent setup"
    title="Models and execution environment"
  >
    <table aria-label="Models and execution environment">
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="align-top">Earlier investigation</th>
          <td>GPT-5.6 Sol <code>(gpt-5.6-sol)</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="align-top">Final diagnosis and fix</th>
          <td>GPT-5.6 Terra <code>(gpt-5.6-terra)</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="align-top">Reasoning effort</th>
          <td>Extra high <code>(xhigh)</code> for both models</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="align-top">Harness</th>
          <td>Codex CLI <code>0.153.4</code>, launched from Zed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="align-top">Sandbox</th>
          <td><.external_link href="https://coop.dryga.com">co:op</.external_link></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="align-top">Codex settings</th>
          <td><code>danger-full-access</code>, approvals <code>never</code> (inside co:op)</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </.field_report_section>
</.field_report>
